.TH NETDOC 1 "2026-09-02" "network-doctor" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
netdoc \- diagnose network connectivity and name the layer where the connection breaks
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B netdoc
.RI [ flags ]
.RI [ target ]
.br
.B netdoc
.BI \-profile " github"
.br
.B netdoc
.BI \-profile " ssh|smtp|web"
.I target
.br
.B netdoc
.BI \-peer\-listen " IP:port"
.RI [ \-peer\-listen " IP:port" ]
.br
.B netdoc
.B \-peer\-connect
.RI [ \-json ]
.br
.B netdoc
.B \-compare
.I before.ndoc after.ndoc
.RI [ \-json ]
.br
.B netdoc
.B \-two\-sided
.I here.ndoc there.ndoc
.RI [ \-json ]
.br
.B netdoc
.B \-two\-sided
.BI \-via " destination"
.RI [ target ]
.RI [ \-json ]
.br
.B netdoc
.BI \-via " destination"
.RI [ flags ]
.RI [ target ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B netdoc
walks the network stack layer by layer and reports, in plain English, the
layer at which the path breaks. With no
.I target
it runs the generic local and internet checks; with a
.I target
it also probes that endpoint.
.PP
Probes run unprivileged and time-bounded, and form a dependency graph with
independent branches, so an unrelated failure never hides a working path.
.PP
By default
.B netdoc
starts a terminal UI. With
.B \-json
it runs headless and prints a machine-readable report instead.
.PP
Flags may be given before or after the
.IR target ,
and each accepts both the single-dash and double-dash spelling
.RB ( \-json
and
.BR \-\-json
are the same flag).
.SH TARGET
.TP
.B example.com
hostname (default port 443)
.TP
.B example.com:8022
hostname with port (protocol inferred from the port)
.TP
.B ssh://example.com:8022
URL (scheme sets protocol and default port; path ignored)
.TP
.BR 192.0.2.1 ", " 2001:db8::1
IP literal
.TP
.B [2001:db8::1]:443
IP literal with port (IPv6 needs the brackets)
.PP
Recognized URL schemes are
.BR http ,
.BR https ,
.BR ssh ,
and
.BR smtp .
At most one target may be given.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-toolbox
Start in toolbox mode, which opens directly on the network tools pane
.RB ( ping ,
.BR dig ,
.BR curl ,
.BR traceroute ,
.BR mtr ,
.BR ss ,
.BR ip ,
.BR nmap )
instead of the diagnosis view. Cannot be combined with
.BR \-json .
.TP
.B \-json
Run the checks headless and print a JSON report on standard output. The field
names and the status vocabulary
.RB ( PASS ,
.BR WARN ,
.BR FAIL ,
.BR SKIP ,
.BR N/A )
are a stable contract, safe to script against.
.TP
.B \-watch
Continuously re-run the checks, starting another pass five seconds after each
one finishes. In the terminal UI this shows the last 20 states and a failure
count per check. Combined with
.BR \-json ,
it streams one compact JSON report per line until the process is interrupted;
those lines carry an extra
.B ts
field (RFC 3339, UTC) and are otherwise the one-shot report unchanged.
.TP
.BI \-profile " name"
Run a built-in service diagnostic plan headlessly. Use
.B \-profile list
to show the available profiles and whether they require a target. Profiles
compose ordinary target runs and preserve each run's checks, findings, causal
evidence, route decisions, and remediation.
.IP
The built-ins are
.BR github ,
which independently tests GitHub web, API, direct SSH on port 22, and alternate
SSH on port 443;
.BR ssh ,
which tests the requested SSH port (22 by default) with DNS, route, TCP,
path-MTU, and banner evidence;
.BR smtp ,
which tests SMTP relay on port 25 and message submission on port 587; and
.BR web ,
which tests HTTPS (443 by default) and plain HTTP on port 80 as independent
paths. Explicit ports
replace the primary port for
.BR ssh ,
.BR smtp ,
and
.BR web .
.IP
Profiles need no terminal. Their text and JSON identify every component and
give one aggregate status and finding. With
.BR \-json ,
the schema is
.BR netdoc.profile-report.v1 .
With
.BR \-save
or
.BR \-support ,
the
.B netdoc.profile.v1
artifact contains a complete
.B netdoc.snapshot.v1
snapshot for every component. Existing single-run snapshots remain unchanged.
.IP
The profile's probes are the minimum selection. Explicit
.B \-check
values are added to every component, and
.B \-skip
then removes named probes and their dependents. A skipped component service
check is reported as
.BR SKIP ;
the aggregate remains
.B WARN
when another component works and becomes
.B FAIL
when none does. The timeout, source interface, public resolver, and support
redaction policy apply independently to every component.
.IP
Combined with
.BR \-watch ,
profiles require
.B \-json
and stream one aggregate object per pass. Profiles cannot be combined with
.BR \-toolbox ,
.BR \-keys ,
peer mode,
.BR \-compare ,
or
.BR \-two-sided .
.TP
.BI \-save " file"
Run the checks headless and write a diagnostic snapshot of the finished run to
.IR file ,
conventionally with a
.B .ndoc
extension. The file is JSON carrying an explicit
.B netdoc.snapshot.v1
schema field, and holds what the run observed and what it concluded: the
target as typed and as parsed, the run settings, every check with its status,
timings and evidence, and the diagnosis. It exists to be reopened, compared,
or attached to a bug report later, without probing anything again.
.IP
Implies headless operation, so it needs no terminal, and it is independent of
.BR \-json ,
which may be given alongside to get the report on standard output as well.
Cannot be combined with
.B \-watch
or
.BR \-toolbox .
The snapshot never changes the diagnosis or the exit code; a snapshot that
cannot be written exits
.BR 2 .
A snapshot contains network information about the machine that ran it,
including addresses, interface names, and the connected Wi\-Fi network name.
It carries no credentials.
.IP
Under
.BR \-profile ,
the file instead uses the separate
.B netdoc.profile.v1
envelope described above. Profile artifacts are not accepted by
.B \-compare
or
.BR \-two-sided ;
those modes compare one target run at a time.
.TP
.BI \-support " file"
Run the checks headless and write a sanitized support snapshot to
.IR file .
This uses the same versioned
.B .ndoc
format as
.BR \-save ,
with explicit redaction metadata. Hostnames, SSIDs, interface and route table
names, the machine's own hostname and account name, paths, URL userinfo and
queries, credential-bearing text, and IP addresses other than public DNS
resolvers are sanitized. Repeated values keep
one pseudonym within the file so routes, DNS answers, and incident states can
still be related. No data is uploaded.
.IP
For a profile artifact, one mapping is shared by every component, so the same
host, interface, address, or route keeps the same pseudonym across the plan.
.IP
Cannot be combined with
.BR \-save ,
.BR \-watch ,
or
.BR \-toolbox .
Only the file is sanitized: a combined
.B \-json
run still prints the ordinary full\-fidelity report on standard output.
The file is readable JSON and should still be inspected before sharing in a
particularly sensitive environment.
.TP
.B \-compare
Compare two diagnostic snapshots written by
.B \-save
or
.B \-support
and report what changed between them. The two files are given as arguments, the
earlier or known-good run first. Runs no probes and opens no socket: everything
reported comes out of the two artifacts, so a comparison works on a machine that
has never seen either network.
.IP
The comparison is semantic rather than a JSON diff. Capture timestamps, probe
durations, and the derived sentences that quote a measurement are not
differences; a set of resolved addresses, a set of connection attempts, and the
.B \-check
selection are compared as sets, so a reordering is not one either. A clock
offset is compared at whole-second resolution. Differences are reported in one
fixed order, so the same two files always produce the same output.
.IP
Comparing snapshots of two different targets is allowed, and the report says so
before anything else, since every row then describes two different endpoints. A
file this build cannot read, including a future
.B schema
version, is refused by name with exit
.BR 2 .
Exit
.B 0
means the two runs describe the same diagnostic state and
.B 1
means they do not; a snapshot recording a failed run is not itself an error
here. Headless, so it needs no terminal, and independent of
.BR \-json ,
which prints the separate
.B netdoc.comparison.v1
schema instead of the table. Cannot be combined with any flag that describes a
run netdoc would perform.
.TP
.B \-two\-sided
Say which of two vantage points a same-target failure is specific to. With two
snapshot arguments, this machine's run is first and the command runs no probes
and opens no socket, the same as
.BR \-compare ,
and asks a different question of the same two files: not what changed between
two runs, but whether a failure belongs to one machine, the other, something
they share, or nowhere the evidence can place it.
.IP
With
.BI \-via " destination"
and zero or one target, start one ordinary diagnosis locally and the equivalent
ordinary diagnosis over SSH concurrently. The local run is side A and the SSH
run is side B. Both completed results use the ordinary canonical snapshot path
and the same two-sided engine as the artifact form. No rendered report text is
parsed and no remote protocol or result schema is added.
.IP
Only rows both machines measured are read. A check that failed on one side and
passed on the other places the failure on that side's vantage point, which
means its own network state, its path, or an endpoint that treats the two
machines differently; it never names a firewall, router, NAT, VPN, or host as
the cause, because two snapshots cannot establish one. Conditions that weaken
the reading, such as a gap between the two captures or settings the runs did
not share, are reported as caveats.
.IP
Two snapshots of different targets are refused with exit
.BR 2 ,
unlike
.BR \-compare ,
which allows them: a row that failed against one host and passed against
another says nothing about which machine is at fault. Exit
.B 0
means no comparable check failed on either machine and
.B 1
means a failure was placed or the evidence could not place one. Headless, so it
needs no terminal, and independent of
.BR \-json ,
which prints the separate
.B netdoc.twosided.v1
schema instead of the table in either form. Live mode applies
.BR \-check ,
.BR \-skip ,
.BR \-timeout ,
and
.B \-public\-dns
equally to both runs. It rejects
.B \-iface
because one machine-specific selector cannot name corresponding interfaces on
two machines. It also rejects
.BR \-profile ,
.BR \-save ,
.BR \-support ,
.BR \-watch ,
.BR \-compare ,
peer mode,
.BR \-toolbox ,
.BR \-no\-history ,
and
.BR \-keys .
The artifact form continues to reject every run setting.
.TP
.BI \-peer\-listen " IP:port"
Open one temporary authenticated peer session on an exact local unicast IP and
port. Port zero asks the kernel to choose an ephemeral port. Repeat once to
offer both IPv4 and IPv6. The command prints a short-lived pairing string,
waits for one peer, runs the two-ended diagnosis, then exits. The advertised
addresses must be directly reachable from the connector.
.TP
.B \-peer\-connect
Read a temporary pairing string from standard input, without placing the
credential in command arguments or target history, then connect and run the
two-ended diagnosis. On a terminal the prompt disables echo. Peer mode is
headless; use
.B \-json
for the separate
.B netdoc.peer.v1
result schema.
.TP
.BI \-via " destination"
Run the checks on an SSH destination instead of on this machine, and present
the finished diagnosis here. The
.I destination
is passed to the system
.BR ssh (1)
client exactly as typed, so
.I ~/.ssh/config
aliases,
.IR user @ host ,
ports, identity files,
.BR ProxyJump ,
and agent authentication all behave as they do for a direct
.B ssh
invocation. netdoc parses no SSH configuration of its own.
.IP
The SSH host must already have a
.B netdoc
on the
.B PATH
of a non-interactive SSH session, new enough to speak the remote protocol.
netdoc installs nothing and changes nothing there. Set
.B NETDOC_VIA_COMMAND
to an explicit path when the executable is not on that
.BR PATH .
Nothing is copied to the SSH host and no shell command is assembled from user
input: the remote command line is a fixed string, and the run settings travel
as structured data on standard input.
.IP
The far end runs the same probes, reaches the same diagnosis, and returns the
same report and snapshot a local run produces, so
.BR \-json ,
.BR \-save ,
and
.B \-support
behave as they always do and the snapshot records the remote machine's
operating system and architecture. An
.B \-iface
value names an interface on the SSH host, and is resolved there.
.IP
With
.BR \-two\-sided ,
one equivalent local run starts concurrently. Its canonical snapshot and the
returned remote snapshot are localized after both complete. In this form
.B \-iface
is rejected rather than silently applying one selector to two different
machines.
.IP
With
.BR \-profile ,
the local process sends one ordinary remote protocol request per profile
component. The profile name is never added to the protocol or a remote shell
command, so a remote netdoc that already speaks protocol version 1 can run the
components even if it predates profiles. The returned ordinary reports and
snapshots are aggregated locally without reinterpreting their findings.
.IP
Implies headless operation, so it needs no terminal. Cannot be combined with
.BR \-toolbox ,
.BR \-watch ,
.BR \-compare ,
or peer mode. The exit code is the remote diagnosis: a check that failed on the
SSH host exits
.BR 1 ,
exactly as it would locally, while an SSH connection or remote protocol failure
exits
.B 2
and says which it was, so the two are never confused.
.TP
.BI \-check " probe[,probe...]"
Run only the named stable probe IDs and the prerequisite closure derived from
the probe DAG. May be repeated; values are combined without changing canonical
probe order. Under
.BR \-profile ,
these probes are added to each component's minimum selection.
.TP
.BI \-skip " probe[,probe...]"
Omit the named stable probe IDs. Dependents whose prerequisite chain is then
unavailable are omitted too. May be repeated and combined with
.BR \-check .
Under
.BR \-profile ,
skip takes precedence over both the profile minimum and explicit checks.
Unknown or empty IDs are rejected before diagnostics start, and the error lists
every valid stable ID. A known ID that does not apply to the current target is
harmless: it selects no row with
.B \-check
and changes nothing with
.BR \-skip .
.TP
.B \-list\-checks
Print every stable probe ID accepted by
.B \-check
and
.BR \-skip ,
followed by its human-readable base name, then exit without running probes.
The order is deterministic. A target or another operational flag is rejected
with exit
.BR 2 .
.TP
.B \-no-reference-egress
Make no connections to netdoc's own built-in reference services. The fixed
internet reference addresses, the captive-portal observation points, the
built-in second-opinion resolver, the fixed QUIC endpoint, the encrypted-DNS
provider, the proxy tunnel's compiled-in destination, and the compiled-in
hostname a targetless run would otherwise resolve are all removed from the
probe DAG, along with any check that depended on one.
.IP
This is not an offline switch. The target you named is still resolved and
connected to, the endpoints of an explicitly selected
.B \-profile
are still contacted, this machine's configured resolver and routing table are
still used, and a
.B \-via
destination is still reached; the remote run then applies the same policy. A
resolver you name with
.B \-public-dns
is your choice rather than netdoc's, so that check still runs; without it the
automatic second opinion does not. Combining this flag with a
.B \-check
that names one of the removed probes is a usage error rather than a silent
override, and exits
.BR 2 .
.TP
.BI \-iface " name\fR|\fPaddress"
Bind probe traffic to an interface name or to an exact local IP address, so
the checks follow a specific link (a VPN tunnel, a second NIC) rather than the
system's default route. Where the native tool supports it, the path drill-downs
follow the same selection:
.BR ping ,
.BR traceroute ,
.BR mtr ,
and
.B curl
bind by interface name on Linux and macOS, or by the selected address when
.B \-\-iface
names an exact IP. Windows tools bind by source address instead. On Windows
.B ping
and
.B tracert
accept a source address for IPv6 destinations only, so an IPv4 run is unbound,
and
.B curl.exe
cannot bind by interface name, though it can use the resolved source address.
Address-only binding follows the literal target's family, then the address
selected by the target TCP check. If neither settles the family, a single-family
selection may be used, but a dual-stack selection is left unbound rather than
guessed. A missing matching source family is also left unbound. The DNS lookup
.RB ( dig ,
.B nslookup
on Windows), the
.B nmap
connect scan, and the SSH and SMTP handshake checks remain unchanged and
unbound. The DNS tools query the system resolver, often a loopback stub, rather
than the target; nmap's
.B \-S
is a spoofing option rather than documented connect-scan binding. Local-state
tools open no sockets, and the LAN scan derives its subnet from the selected
probe source address.
.TP
.BI \-public\-dns " address"
IP address of the resolver used for the second-opinion DNS check, which is
queried directly to compare its answer against the system resolver's. Must be
an IP literal, IPv4 or IPv6 \(em a hostname is rejected, since resolving it
would defeat the purpose of the check. The default is
.BR 8.8.8.8 .
An empty value
.RB ( \-\-public\-dns
.BR \(dq\(dq )
drops the check entirely: the row disappears from the report and no query
leaves the machine, which is what a strict egress policy or a privacy
requirement calls for.
.TP
.B \-no\-history
Neither read nor write the saved target history for this run, so the targets
typed in the terminal UI stay in that session only. An existing history file is
left untouched; see
.B FILES
for where it lives and how to clear it.
.TP
.BI \-keys " preset"
Keybinding preset for the terminal UI, either
.B default
or
.BR vim .
The
.B vim
preset adds
.BR gg / G
for first and last,
.BR ctrl+b / ctrl+f
for page up and down, and
.BR ctrl+u / ctrl+d
for half-page up and down inside a tool job's output; every default key keeps
working. An empty value selects
.BR default ;
any other value is rejected. There are no other presets and no keybinding
configuration file.
.TP
.BI \-timeout " duration"
Per-check probe timeout, as a Go duration string such as
.B 2s
or
.BR 1500ms .
Must be positive. The default is
.BR 4s .
.TP
.B \-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-help ", " \-h
Print usage and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The chain completed with no failed row, or peer traffic passed in both
directions. Skipped rows are allowed. Under
.B \-compare
it means the two snapshots describe the same diagnostic state. Under
.B \-two\-sided
it means no comparable check failed on either side.
.TP
.B 1
A row failed, the terminal UI was quit before the chain finished, or the run
itself errored. A failed, ambiguous, or incomplete peer diagnosis also exits
with this status. Under
.B \-json \-watch
this is the last completed pass's status. Under
.B \-compare
it means the two snapshots differ, which is an answer rather than a failure.
Under
.B \-two\-sided
it means a failure was placed or the evidence could not place one.
.TP
.B 2
Bad arguments, a target or pairing input the parser rejected, a snapshot
.B \-compare
could not read, or no terminal on standard output to run the terminal UI on.
Under
.B \-via
it also means the diagnosis never happened: the SSH connection failed, no
usable
.B netdoc
ran on the SSH host, or the two ends could not agree on the remote protocol. A
remote run that did happen and found a broken network exits
.BR 1 .
Under live
.BR \-two\-sided ,
different effective targets and remote acquisition failures also exit
.BR 2 .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NETDOC_VIA_COMMAND
The
.B netdoc
executable to start on the SSH host under
.BR \-via ,
read on the local machine and defaulting to
.BR netdoc .
Use it when the SSH host's installation is not on the
.B PATH
of a non-interactive session. The value must be a plain path with no spaces or
shell punctuation, so that it means the same thing to every remote shell.
.SH PEER SECURITY AND REACHABILITY
Each listening run creates a new Ed25519 TLS certificate and a random 256-bit
session token. The pairing string carries direct endpoint addresses, the
certificate's SHA-256 pin, the token, protocol version, and expiration. TLS 1.3
encrypts the exchange; the pin authenticates the listener and the token
authenticates the connector. Requests use fresh nonces and one session accepts
only a bounded number of messages and connections. Credentials exist only in
memory and in the pairing string, and are excluded from reports and errors.
.PP
The peer exchanges host names, short-lived listener addresses, actual socket
addresses, connection timing, TCP/TLS/application phase outcomes, and the
combined diagnosis. Review this network metadata before sharing a JSON result.
.PP
There is no relay, rendezvous service, account, automatic port forwarding, or
NAT traversal. At least one listener address must be directly reachable. A
failed reverse connection can be consistent with filtering, routing, or NAT,
but does not prove which one caused it.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Run the generic local and internet diagnosis:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
netdoc
.EE
.RE
.PP
Diagnose the path to a host, over HTTP, TLS, and HTTPS:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
netdoc github.com
.EE
.RE
.PP
Diagnose GitHub's independent service paths, or an SSH server with its route,
path-MTU, and banner evidence:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
netdoc \-\-profile github
netdoc \-\-profile ssh server.example.com
netdoc \-\-via ideapad \-\-profile github \-\-json
.EE
.RE
.PP
Let the port select the protocol rows, here the SSH banner check:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
netdoc github.com:22
.EE
.RE
.PP
Keep SSH on a nonstandard port with an explicit scheme:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
netdoc ssh://host:2222
.EE
.RE
.PP
Diagnose a remote machine's network from here, through an
.I ~/.ssh/config
alias:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
netdoc \-\-via server example.com
netdoc \-\-via server \-\-json \-\-check dns,target_tcp example.com
netdoc \-\-via server \-\-save remote.ndoc example.com
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run the same target locally and over SSH, then localize their differential, or
perform the same reading later from two saved snapshots:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
netdoc \-\-two\-sided \-\-via ideapad example.com
netdoc \-\-two\-sided \-\-via ideapad \-\-json example.com
netdoc \-\-two\-sided here.ndoc there.ndoc
.EE
.RE
.PP
Get one JSON report on standard output, for scripts, CI, or a bug report:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
netdoc \-\-json github.com
.EE
.RE
.PP
Follow an intermittent failure as it happens:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
netdoc \-\-json \-\-watch github.com | jq \-c 'select(.ok | not) | {ts, failed_stage, summary}'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Select a narrow diagnostic branch, or omit unrelated egress probes:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
netdoc \-\-check dns,target_tcp,tls example.com
netdoc \-\-skip internet_tcp,quic_udp_443 example.com
netdoc \-\-check tls \-\-skip target_tcp example.com
.EE
.RE
.PP
Send the probes out through a specific interface:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
netdoc \-\-iface wg0 github.com
.EE
.RE
.PP
Take the second opinion from a different resolver, or drop it altogether:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
netdoc \-\-public\-dns 9.9.9.9 github.com
netdoc \-\-public\-dns "" github.com
.EE
.RE
.PP
Save a run now, and later ask what changed:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
netdoc \-\-save good.ndoc github.com
netdoc \-\-save bad.ndoc github.com
netdoc \-\-compare good.ndoc bad.ndoc
.EE
.RE
.PP
Offer a temporary direct peer session, then connect from the other machine:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
netdoc \-\-peer\-listen 192.168.1.20:4242
netdoc \-\-peer\-connect
.EE
.RE
.PP
Use the exit status in a shell script:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
netdoc github.com || echo "path to github is broken"
.EE
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/netdoc/history
Up to 50 recent targets, saved by the terminal UI between sessions. Normally
.IR ~/.config/netdoc/history .
On macOS this is
.IR ~/Library/Application\ Support/netdoc/history ,
and on Windows
.IR %AppData%\enetdoc\ehistory .
.B \-\-no\-history
skips the file entirely for one run, without removing it. Exit
.B netdoc
and delete the file to clear what is already saved.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR netdoc\-sim (1),
.BR ping (8),
.BR dig (1),
.BR curl (1),
.BR traceroute (8),
.BR mtr (8),
.BR ss (8),
.BR ip (8),
.BR nmap (1),
.BR ssh (1)
.PP
